# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Verte — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is the template
# and must never hold a real secret.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# TWO HALVES, AND THE SPLIT MATTERS:
#
#   REACT_APP_*  is compiled INTO the extension bundle. Anyone who installs
#                the extension can read these. Public values only.
#   everything else is read by the proxy, server-side. Secrets live here and
#                only here — see BUILD_PLAN.md §08, "The eBay secret cannot
#                live in the extension".
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ══ EXTENSION (public — shipped in the bundle) ═══════════════════════════════

# Where the extension sends its lookups. Point this at the proxy once it's up;
# until then the legacy Bubble backend keeps the popup working.
# Local proxy:  http://localhost:8787
# Deployed:     https://verte-proxy.<you>.workers.dev
REACT_APP_VERTE_API_BASE=https://greenbeans.bubbleapps.io/version-test/api/1.1/obj

# Where "How we pick listings" goes. Your repo README, or the devpost page.
REACT_APP_METHODOLOGY_URL=https://github.com/verte-extension/verte#methodology


# ══ PROXY (secret — never prefixed REACT_APP_) ═══════════════════════════════

# ── eBay Browse API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# developer.ebay.com → sign in → My Account → Application Keysets.
# Start the signup FIRST (BUILD_PLAN.md §03) — approval can eat ninety minutes.
# Each keyset gives you an App ID (client id) and a Cert ID (client secret).
# Use the Sandbox keyset to wire things up, Production for the demo: sandbox
# has almost no real listings, and an empty card demos badly.
# PARKED — no keyset in time for the event. The working Verte build reads
# secondhand listings from the retailer page itself instead, no key needed.
# EBAY_CLIENT_ID=
# EBAY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# sandbox | production — must match the keyset above
# EBAY_ENV=production

# ── OpenAI (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used for ONE job: when the keyword rules in proxy/categories.js return no
# match, ask a model which of the 24 known slugs the title belongs to. It is a
# fallback behind the rules, never in front of them — deterministic first, so a
# misclassification stays fixable in five seconds at 3am (BUILD_PLAN.md §08).
#
# It never sources listings, prices, URLs or carbon figures. A model will
# happily produce a plausible listing that does not exist, and one dead link in
# the demo is the whole demo.
#
# Leave empty to run on keyword rules alone — everything works without it.
# Key from platform.openai.com → API keys (starts "sk-").
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# ── Snowflake ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Key-pair auth is what the working Verte build uses.
# Key-pair auth (SNOWFLAKE_JWT), not a password — the key file stays on disk
# and only its path lives here.
SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT=
SNOWFLAKE_USER=
SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=
SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE=
SNOWFLAKE_ROLE=
SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE=
SNOWFLAKE_SCHEMA=
# Password auth is unused while a key path is set. Leave empty.
SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD=

# ── Proxy server ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8787
# Which extension may call the proxy. `chrome-extension://<id>`, where the id
# is on chrome://extensions once the unpacked build is loaded. `*` is fine
# during the event; tighten it before anything ships.
ALLOWED_ORIGIN=*
